course: dictionary
lesson: 1865
title: Chance or a plan?
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A coincidence is when two things happen at the same time, or in a surprising way, by chance. Nobody planned it. When you bump into a friend at the shops, you might say, «What a coincidence!»
explain: Read the words around the word to find the clues. «That's not a coincidence: worry often shows up in the tummy before you even notice it in your head.» Here the writer says it is NOT just chance. Ollie's tummy ache happened every time Theo said the same thing, so the two are linked.
explain: Ask yourself: was it planned, or did it just happen by chance? If the answer is "by chance", it is a coincidence. In maths, a pattern that looks like a link may also be a coincidence, so you need many tests before you can be sure.
fact: A coincidence is when things happen together by chance, without being planned.
fact: «By coincidence» means it happened by chance, and «pure coincidence» means it was only chance.
fact: «Emoji» is Japanese, and its likeness to «emotion» is a coincidence: the two words just look alike.
fact: Barter needs a «double coincidence of wants», which means each person has what the other person wants, at the same time.
fact: In maths, a pattern can be a coincidence, not proof; you need many tests to be sure.
exercise: The writer says the likeness of «emoji» and «emotion» is a coincidence. What does this mean? | It was planned by the Japanese makers | It proves the two words come from the same place | It means the two words have the same meaning | *It is just chance, not planned
exercise: «That's not a coincidence: worry often shows up in the tummy.» What does the writer mean? | Theo planned the tummy ache | *It is not chance; the two things are linked | It was just luck, with no reason | It happened only once
exercise: What does «by coincidence» mean? | In a loud, angry voice | Very quickly and suddenly | On purpose, with a careful plan | *By chance, not planned
test: Zara bumps into her cousin in a city far from home, and neither of them planned it. What is this? | A plan | A rule | A sum | *A coincidence
test: In maths, a pattern appears once and looks like a link. Why should you not be sure it is a rule? | Because patterns are never real | *It may be a coincidence, so you need many tests | Because one test always proves a rule | Because maths has no tests
exam: Which one is a coincidence? | A sum that always gives the same answer | Ollie plans a party for Theo every Friday | A rule that everyone must follow | *Two friends buy the same red bike on the same day, by chance
